1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,402
Can two similar narrowings affect blood
flow differently?

2
00:00:03,913 --> 00:00:07,326
Yes. A picture shows the shape of the
artery.

3
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:11,773
Pressure measurements give the care team
another way to assess how much a narrowing

4
00:00:11,842 --> 00:00:12,562
matters.

5
00:00:13,166 --> 00:00:17,983
A coronary angiogram uses contrast and
X-rays to show the channel

6
00:00:18,076 --> 00:00:22,349
inside the artery. The clinician can see
where it is narrow.

7
00:00:22,953 --> 00:00:28,061
In selected cases, it is unclear how much
that narrowing affects blood supply to the

8
00:00:28,119 --> 00:00:32,124
heart muscle. That is where a pressure-
based test may help.

9
00:00:32,728 --> 00:00:35,433
A thin wire carries a pressure sensor.

10
00:00:35,944 --> 00:00:39,345
In this example, the sensor is beyond the
narrowing.

11
00:00:40,042 --> 00:00:44,814
The team compares pressure there with
pressure near the artery’s opening, using

12
00:00:44,872 --> 00:00:47,077
the aortic pressure as a reference.

13
00:00:47,774 --> 00:00:52,499
The comparison helps estimate how strongly
the narrowing limits blood flow under the

14
00:00:52,534 --> 00:00:53,892
conditions of the test.

15
00:00:54,844 --> 00:00:58,118
F F R stands for fractional flow reserve.

16
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:02,786
It uses the pressure comparison when blood
flow has been increased as much as

17
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:04,179
possible for the test.

18
00:01:04,876 --> 00:01:07,941
The team uses a medicine to create those
conditions.

19
00:01:08,382 --> 00:01:12,294
The measurement helps estimate how the
narrowing affects flow when the artery

20
00:01:12,364 --> 00:01:13,804
needs to carry more.

21
00:01:14,756 --> 00:01:19,017
I F R stands for instantaneous wave-free
ratio.

22
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:24,230
It uses the pressure comparison at rest,
during a particular part of each heartbeat

23
00:01:24,265 --> 00:01:25,820
when the heart is relaxing.

24
00:01:26,262 --> 00:01:28,897
The measurement uses that resting window.

25
00:01:29,408 --> 00:01:33,773
Both tests use pressure to help the team
judge the effect of a narrowing.

26
00:01:34,214 --> 00:01:38,510
The team checks that the pressure signals
are reliable and interprets them with the

27
00:01:38,556 --> 00:01:42,016
symptoms, the artery anatomy and other
findings.

28
00:01:42,713 --> 00:01:47,809
If the assessment suggests the narrowing
is unlikely to limit blood supply, a stent

29
00:01:47,937 --> 00:01:52,627
may be deferred. Another result may
support a treatment discussion when it

30
00:01:52,697 --> 00:01:54,404
fits the clinical picture.

31
00:01:55,007 --> 00:02:00,185
These measurements focus on the effect of
a narrowing in a major coronary artery.

32
00:02:00,998 --> 00:02:03,378
Chest symptoms can have other causes.

33
00:02:03,761 --> 00:02:08,417
If the explanation is still unclear, the
care team may consider another test.

34
00:02:08,928 --> 00:02:13,746
The goal is to choose a next step that
fits the person and the findings together.

35
00:02:14,257 --> 00:02:18,065
If your team uses one of these
measurements, ask what it showed about the

36
00:02:18,135 --> 00:02:20,828
narrowing and how that changes the next
step.

37
00:02:21,641 --> 00:02:23,533
The angiogram supplies the map.

38
00:02:24,044 --> 00:02:29,396
F F R and I F R add pressure information
that can help guide the decision.
